A||||7||msweldon||better SCCA engine?||||1||
Z||000000||msweldon||07-23-2001||10:35 AM||||I know that SCCA racing is mostly the driver but my stock v6 is on its last legs and I'm looking to move up in the power world.<P>I currently have a 3.4TDC that I'm getting ready to place into my 87gt.  This engine will add about 90lbs to the rear of the car, yes aluminum head but the HUGE heads and cam carriers add alot.  <P>Will this 90lbs be compensatable on the track?<P>Also this would throw me into the open modified class correct?  <P>After looking at the competition in the open class, it doesn't look too friendly  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I've also considered going with a V8 archie kit with a ZZ04 LT1 with aluminum heads that only adds ~90lbs over the stock v6 setup as well. For those who have raced scca do any of you race v8 fieros and if so...pros??cons???  <P>Suggestions and Input needed....<P>thanks in advance<P>msweldon||||reg||1
Z||000001||AutoXer||07-23-2001||11:00 AM||||I think that I have seen 4.3L V6 Fieros racing in the GT classes.  I'm not sure which GT class.  Any of these are big $$$ classes to be competative in.  I'd suggest rebuilding your V6 if it is tired and stay stock.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Will||07-23-2001||12:10 PM||||I can't imagine that the 3400TDC would add 90 lbs. That's the same as the weight gain from a SBC!<P>Anyway, are you road racing or AutoXing? If your region has adopted the new Street Modified 2-seater class you might compete in that against supercharged Integras and B18 Civics...||||reg||1
Z||000003||msweldon||07-23-2001||12:57 PM||||Will,<P>no kidding about the extra 90lbs...the heads on the thing are just enormous, I'll post a pic when my dig camera comes in but in the meantime look at <A HREF="http://www.fiero.cc" TARGET=_blank>www.fiero.cc</A>  and look at Micheal smith's page.<P>I'm still very new to SCCA.  As of late I'll just show up at a scheduled event and watch usually they'll let me run for a small fee after their competition is over and they start the 1$ passenger run's.  So I guess that would be just autocross, correct?  Most of the time the tracks are too tight for any advantage with larger engines.  At the last race, a 914 w/ 150hp stomped a 280z with a sbc and ~300hp who was in turn stomped by a go-kart from a local "Malibu" raceway franchise.<P>I use my fiero as a part time daily driver and I want real sports car acceleration but I don't want to mess up the handling characteristics.<P>I've got the stock suspension already as moded as possible without going to new a-arms, e.g. konis, steel bushings on cradle and rear a-arms, poly bushing up front, sus-tech springs, 16"'s front 17"'s rear, speedway brake setup too.<P>Anyway, my real question is what class would I be in if I went with my 220hp DOHC vs a sbc say from archie?<P>thanks all,<BR>marc||||reg||1
Z||000004||Will||07-23-2001||03:43 PM||||Weigh the engine, don't just guess by how big it is. Those heads are aluminum, remember? A Northstar is larger than a Small Block Chevy, but lighter too.<P>Course set up in traffic cones on a parking lot? Yeah, those are autocrosses.<P>If your region has adopted the new street modified 2-seat class (SM2), then you could probably run in SM2 against the cars I mentioned before: turbo B18 civics, turbo or supercharged integras, etc.<BR>If not, you'd be in D- or E-modified.||||reg||1
Z||000005||msweldon||07-23-2001||05:38 PM||||Will,<P>Yes you are correct about the n* vs sbc and the primary reasoning, so i've read, is that aluminum engines must have more volume to prevent warping and the such versus a iron block.  <P>Aluminum has approx. 40% less weight than cast iron given the same volume, correct? Well the heads on the 3.4dohc are 4-5 times the size of the standard 2.8 heads and they both share more or less the same block.<P>I've just been going on what Micheal smith told me, who got it from another source.<P>I'll find out for sure either tonight or tomorrow and weight the engine on my cherry picker.<P>What do you call the hanging thingies that you can weigh very large object via a lift or picker?  I'll go buy one shortly.<P>mw||||reg||1
Z||000006||87FieroGTx||07-23-2001||06:09 PM|||| For SCCA I would recomend the Quad 4 with a turbo and 4 speed manual. This setup has 325hp and put weight distrabution at 50/50.<P> Or if you want to stay stockish Go with a built 3.2 you can git a good 200hp out of a n/a 3.2 or go with a turbo and bring it up to 280hp.<P> I don't know the rules but you may be able to git away with a 3.4 block. Built up you can git 230hp n/a without a prob, with turbo 310hp.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Shiner||07-23-2001||06:30 PM||||I say put a 3.4 pushrod in there, bolt up all the Fiero longblock stuff, and don't tell ANYBODY about it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  Just kidding, cheating is bad, or so I have heard.<P>------------------<BR>-Shiner-<BR>'84 Sport Coupe<BR>'87 GT<BR>'86 SE 2M6 (parts car)||||reg||1
